sentences.t:
ἦ μένει εἰς ὅ κε δὴ νῆες θοαὶ ἄγχι θαλάσσης
Ἀργείων ἀέκητι πυρὸς δηΐοιο θέρωνται,
αὐτοί τε κτεινώμεθ’ ἐπισχερώ;

sentences.l:
when I killed Itymoneus,
the good son of Hypeirochus, who in Elis used to dwell,
driving off goods in reprisal;

For to him too a great debt was owed in heavenly Elis:
four prize-bearing horses, with the very chariots,
that had gone to the contests;

And there is a certain Thryoessa, a city, a steep hill,
far off on the Alpheus, the furthest of sandy Pylos;
this they were encamped around, eager to raze it.

But me Neleus
did not allow to arm, and he hid from me my horses;
for he said that I did not yet know at all the works of war.

But when between the Pylians and the Epeans the quarrel arose,
first I took a man, and took charge of the solid-hoofed horses,
Mulius, the spearman;

until to Buprasium, rich in wheat, we made our horses go,
and to the Olenian rock, and where the hill of Alesium
is called;
